A painterly animated romantic drama follows a young courier and a young mechanic racing through a decaying galactic port during their final minutes together. The Courier wears a weathered crimson travel coat and carries one small flight bag; the Mechanic wears a dark work jacket marked by luminous machine dust. Keep their faces, clothing, and bag consistent throughout.

Use an Arcane-inspired visual style: sculpted 3D characters finished with expressive hand-painted textures, visible brushwork, graphic silhouettes, fractured colored light, atmospheric depth, painterly motion smears, selective frame skipping, and explosive fluidity during major movements. Build a lived-in galactic frontier of patched starships, analog machines, strange travelers, service automatons, rain-dark metal, and monumental industrial architecture. Use deep blue shadows, corroded copper, crimson fabric, cyan engines, and warm gold skin light.

The conversation begins on the first frame and ends on the final frame. Every timed beat contains dialogue. The Courier and Mechanic speak directly to each other in natural American English, with intimate restraint and precise lip sync. Carry a breath or vocal overlap across each cut so the conversation never restarts or leaves an empty pause.

0-4 seconds: A magnetic tram races above an ocean city built vertically into black cliffs beneath an immense ringed planet. The Courier and Mechanic stand on its exposed rear platform as rain bends horizontally and starships rise around them. The Courier looks across the impossible skyline rather than at him: {This city only looks beautiful when we’re escaping it.}

4-9 seconds: The tram tears through a crowded docking tower without stopping. They jump together onto a suspended freight platform moving in the opposite direction, landing hard among swaying cargo and startled alien travelers. The Mechanic catches her before she falls: {We spent years dreaming of this, and now you’re leaving alone.}

9-13 seconds: They enter a transparent customs sphere just as its artificial gravity fails. Travelers, luggage, droplets, and service machines drift upward. The couple floats apart until the Courier catches his sleeve and pulls herself close enough to meet his eyes: {You had years to give me reasons to stay.}

13-17 seconds: Gravity returns. Hard cut on their impact to a magnetic elevator dropping through the center of a colossal shipyard. Half-built vessels and welding constellations streak upward outside the glass. The Mechanic watches their reflections standing together instead of looking at her: {I thought loving you quietly might be enough.}

17-21 seconds: The elevator opens inside a cathedral-sized orbital greenhouse suspended between two towers. Turbulence from an arriving ship tears thousands of luminous seed petals into a spiraling current. They push through the glowing storm, the Courier running backward for a moment so she can face him: {I waited for you to choose me out loud.}

21-25 seconds: They emerge onto the greenhouse roof as a narrow service arm rotates above the city to meet her departing skiff. The couple steps onto the moving arm together, surrounded by rain, drifting petals, and the immense ocean far below. As the arm carries them toward the ship, the Mechanic finally holds her gaze: {I chose you every day, just never bravely enough.}

25-30 seconds: The service arm aligns with the skiff for only a moment. The Courier steps aboard while the Mechanic remains on the arm; they keep their hands linked as the skiff immediately begins to rise. Distance slowly pulls their fingers apart. Suspended above the glowing city, she calls through the wind: {Then say it before the sky takes me.} The Mechanic runs to the end of the arm as the skiff climbs beyond his reach. He finally shouts: {I love you.} She hears him, but the clouds swallow the skiff before she can answer. Cut to black on his final word.

No BGM. Use only dialogue, rain, tram vibration, cargo impacts, crowd voices, gravity-system pulses, elevator resonance, rushing petals, rotating machinery, engines, breath, and wind. Keep environmental sound beneath the voices. No subtitles, captions, logos, title cards, or graphic overlays.

